## Understanding the Problem

When you click on a textarea, the default behavior is to place the cursor at the click position. The `select()` method selects all the text, but on subsequent clicks, the browser's default behavior (placing the cursor) happens after the `select()` call, effectively deselecting the text.

## Solution

To ensure the text is selected on every click, we need to call `select()` after the browser's default click behavior has been processed. We can do this by using `setTimeout` with a delay of 0, which pushes the `select()` call to the end of the event queue.

## Updated JavaScript Code

```javascript
const textarea = document.getElementById('my-textarea');

textarea.addEventListener('click', () => {
  setTimeout(() => textarea.select(), 0);
});
```

By using `setTimeout`, we ensure that `select()` is called after the browser has finished processing the click event, including placing the cursor. This way, the text will be selected on every click.

## Alternative Solution

Another approach is to use the `focus` event instead of the `click` event. The `focus` event fires when the textarea receives focus, which happens when you click on it. However, this might not work as expected if the textarea already has focus and you click inside it again.

```javascript
